module sysl.sync

// A word two threads may touch at once, and the operations that touch it indivisibly.
//
// The operations underneath are the language's -- nine forms in the raw tier, each one machine
// instruction (`library/sync.md`) -- and every one of them takes an **address**. So this is an
// ordinary struct with a field and methods that take the address of it, which is the whole of what
// "built on `*T`" means, and it is why the type a program reaches for lives here in the library
// where a reader can see what it is made of rather than inside the compiler where they cannot.
//
// **`T` is an integer of 8, 16, 32 or 64 bits, or a pointer** -- what the machine has an instruction
// for. Nothing here declares a bound saying so, because there is no trait that means "a width a
// processor can touch indivisibly": sysl's integers are an open family, so `u12` is a type a program
// may name and no `impl` list could have covered. What refuses `Atomic[u12]` is the form inside,
// where the instruction would have had to be chosen, and it names the widths in its message. The
// arithmetic members are refused on a pointer for the same kind of reason and in the same place.
//
// **Reading `a.v` directly is an ordinary non-atomic read.** The field is not hidden, because a
// thread that knows it is alone with the value -- the one that built it, the one left after every
// other has been joined -- is entitled to the cheap read, and hiding it would only mean a method
// that does the same thing less visibly. It is a race everywhere else, and it is greppable, which is
// the bargain the whole `*T` tier makes.
struct Atomic[T]

    // The value itself. See the note above on reading it without going through a method.
    v: T

    // Reads the word.
    //
    // The receiver is `*self` rather than `self` for a reason worth stating, since a read looks like
    // it should not need one: a `self` receiver is handed a **copy** of the struct, and the address
    // of a copy is not the address the other threads are writing to. It would compile, it would be
    // atomic, and it would read the wrong word.
    //
    // **Three of the five orderings apply**, and the contract is what says so. A release publishes
    // the writes that came before it and a load makes none, so `Release` and the release half of
    // `AcqRel` describe a load that cannot exist -- the machine has no such instruction and the form
    // underneath refuses both by name. What the form cannot refuse is a *variable*, which is what
    // arrives here, so the check is a `require` and lands at run time. It costs nothing where the
    // ordering was written at the call, which is every ordinary use: the branch is over a constant
    // and folds away with the dispatch below it.
    load(*self, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T
        require ord.orders_a_load(), "a load has nothing to release"

        ord match
            Relaxed -> atomic_load(&self.v, Relaxed)
            Acquire -> atomic_load(&self.v, Acquire)
            _       -> atomic_load(&self.v, SeqCst)
    end load

    // Writes the word.
    //
    // **Three of the five orderings apply**, for the mirror of the reason on `load`: a store reads
    // nothing, so there is nothing for it to acquire.
    store(*self, v: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst)
        require ord.orders_a_store(), "a store has nothing to acquire"

        ord match
            Relaxed -> atomic_store(&self.v, v, Relaxed)
            Release -> atomic_store(&self.v, v, Release)
            _       -> atomic_store(&self.v, v, SeqCst)
    end store

    // Writes the word and answers what was there before.
    swap(*self, v: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T = ord match
        Relaxed -> atomic_swap(&self.v, v, Relaxed)
        Acquire -> atomic_swap(&self.v, v, Acquire)
        Release -> atomic_swap(&self.v, v, Release)
        AcqRel  -> atomic_swap(&self.v, v, AcqRel)
        SeqCst  -> atomic_swap(&self.v, v, SeqCst)

    // Writes `desired` if the word is `expected`, and answers the value it **found** either way.
    //
    // A caller learns whether it swapped by comparing the answer against what they expected, which
    // is one comparison they were going to make anyway -- and on failure the answer is the value to
    // retry against, so the loop reads `while a.cas(seen, f(seen)) != seen`.
    cas(*self, expected: T, desired: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T = ord match
        Relaxed -> atomic_cas(&self.v, expected, desired, Relaxed)
        Acquire -> atomic_cas(&self.v, expected, desired, Acquire)
        Release -> atomic_cas(&self.v, expected, desired, Release)
        AcqRel  -> atomic_cas(&self.v, expected, desired, AcqRel)
        SeqCst  -> atomic_cas(&self.v, expected, desired, SeqCst)

    // Adds to the word and answers what was there before, which is what makes an atomic increment
    // usable as a ticket: every caller gets a different number and none of them is skipped.
    add(*self, n: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T = ord match
        Relaxed -> atomic_add(&self.v, n, Relaxed)
        Acquire -> atomic_add(&self.v, n, Acquire)
        Release -> atomic_add(&self.v, n, Release)
        AcqRel  -> atomic_add(&self.v, n, AcqRel)
        SeqCst  -> atomic_add(&self.v, n, SeqCst)

    // Subtracts from the word and answers what was there before.
    sub(*self, n: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T = ord match
        Relaxed -> atomic_sub(&self.v, n, Relaxed)
        Acquire -> atomic_sub(&self.v, n, Acquire)
        Release -> atomic_sub(&self.v, n, Release)
        AcqRel  -> atomic_sub(&self.v, n, AcqRel)
        SeqCst  -> atomic_sub(&self.v, n, SeqCst)

    // Bitwise-ands into the word and answers what was there before.
    and(*self, n: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T = ord match
        Relaxed -> atomic_and(&self.v, n, Relaxed)
        Acquire -> atomic_and(&self.v, n, Acquire)
        Release -> atomic_and(&self.v, n, Release)
        AcqRel  -> atomic_and(&self.v, n, AcqRel)
        SeqCst  -> atomic_and(&self.v, n, SeqCst)

    // Bitwise-ors into the word and answers what was there before, which is how a flag is set in a
    // word other threads are setting their own flags in.
    or(*self, n: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T = ord match
        Relaxed -> atomic_or(&self.v, n, Relaxed)
        Acquire -> atomic_or(&self.v, n, Acquire)
        Release -> atomic_or(&self.v, n, Release)
        AcqRel  -> atomic_or(&self.v, n, AcqRel)
        SeqCst  -> atomic_or(&self.v, n, SeqCst)

    // Bitwise-xors into the word and answers what was there before.
    xor(*self, n: T, ord: Ordering = SeqCst) -> T = ord match
        Relaxed -> atomic_xor(&self.v, n, Relaxed)
        Acquire -> atomic_xor(&self.v, n, Acquire)
        Release -> atomic_xor(&self.v, n, Release)
        AcqRel  -> atomic_xor(&self.v, n, AcqRel)
        SeqCst  -> atomic_xor(&self.v, n, SeqCst)

end Atomic

// **A fence has no wrapper here, and the omission is deliberate.** `atomic_fence(ord)` is the fence;
// it is a barrier the whole thread passes through rather than an operation on any one location, so
// there is no value for it to be a member of and nothing for a struct to hold. What a free function
// beside it could add is the default -- and the default is what it could not survive. `Relaxed` is
// **refused** by the form, because a fence is nothing but its ordering and a relaxed one would be a
// barrier that orders nothing; a wrapper taking `Ordering` would need a `Relaxed` arm, and the only
// two things that arm could do are call a form that refuses it or quietly do nothing. Softening the
// diagnostic is worse than writing `Acquire` out, so the form is what a program writes.
